    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Modified_Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    nf = 5\n    nc = 14\n    facility_coords = [(77, 66), (19, 5), (67, 29), (16, 12), (91, 22)]\n    customer_coords = [(98, 87), (46, 15), (38, 38), (34, 77), (0, 27), (52, 47), (38, 8), (5, 38), (63, 52), (84, 22), (2, 4), (6, 18), (27, 23), (64, 24)]\n    capacities = [115.0, 198.0, 133.0, 115.0, 195.0]\n    demands = [26.0, 21.0, 28.0, 45.0, 22.0, 23.0, 33.0, 35.0, 39.0, 34.0, 28.0, 50.0, 23.0, 34.0]\n    fixed_costs = [223.0, 365.0, 234.0, 386.0, 298.0]\n    rate = 0.5\n\n    # Compute distances\n    dist = {}\n    for i in range(nf):\n        for j in range(nc):\n            dx = facility_coords[i][0] - customer_coords[j][0]\n            dy = facility_coords[i][1] - customer_coords[j][1]\n            dist[(i,j)] = math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)\n\n    # Variables\n    open_f = {i: m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f'Open_F{i}') for i in range(nf)}\n    ship = {(i,j): m.addVar(lb=0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f'Ship_F{i}_C{j}')\n            for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc)}\n\n    # Objective\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf))\n    obj += quicksum(dist[(i,j)]*rate*ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc))\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction\n    for j in range(nc):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf)) >= demands[j])\n\n    # Capacity linking\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= capacities[i]*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: F0 requires F2\n    m.addConstr(open_f[0] <= open_f[2])\n\n    # Policy: at most 3 facilities open\n    m.addConstr(quicksum(open_f[i] for i in range(nf)) <= 3)\n\n    # Policy: no facility serves > 58% of total demand\n    total_demand = 441.0\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= 0.58*total_demand)\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Distribution Network Decision \u2014 Warehouse Activation and Customer Allocation\n\nPurpose\nThis memo outlines the decision framework to determine which candidate warehouses to activate and how to allocate shipments to customers so that every order is fulfilled at the lowest possible total cost while adhering to company policies. The analysis integrates fixed opening costs and distance- and volume-driven transportation spending, together with site capacity limits and known customer demand.\n\nBusiness Requirements and Policies\n- Service commitments: Each customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied within the planning horizon. No unmet demand is allowed.\n- Activation rule: A candidate warehouse may ship product only if it is activated. Activating a site incurs its fixed opening cost.\n- Capacity discipline: Each warehouse has a defined throughput limit. The total outbound volume assigned to any activated site must not exceed its available capacity.\n- Transport economics: Freight spending is driven by distance and volume, based on a uniform transport rate. Distances will be derived consistently from the map coordinates provided.\n- Hub dependency: The candidate location designated F0 depends on the regional hub designated F2. If F0 is activated, F2 must also be activated for the duration of the plan.\n- Management span of control: The company will operate at most 3 distribution centers at any given time due to management oversight capacity.\n- Resilience posture: To avoid over-reliance on any single facility, no distribution center may fulfill more than 58% of the total system demand across all customers.\n- Cost goal: The decision should minimize the all-in network cost, defined as the sum of fixed activation costs and transportation spend, while meeting all of the above requirements.\n\nDecision Approach\n- Evaluate feasible combinations of candidate warehouses, honoring the management cap on the number of active sites and the F0\u2013F2 hub relationship.\n- For each feasible activation set, assign customer volumes so that:\n  - Every customer\u2019s full demand is met.\n  - No warehouse is loaded beyond its capacity.\n  - The share of total system demand served by any one site stays within the stated limit.\n  - Shipments originate only from activated sites.\n- Distances between warehouses and customers will be derived from the provided coordinates, and transportation cost will be calculated using the uniform rate. The resulting total cost for each activation and allocation plan will combine transportation spend with the relevant fixed opening costs.\n- Where beneficial for cost or required for policy compliance, customer demand may be split across more than one activated warehouse.\n\nDecision Criteria and Deliverables\n- Select the activation and shipment plan with the lowest total cost that meets all service, capacity, dependency, management, and resilience policies.\n- Provide a clear recommendation specifying:\n  - Which warehouses to activate (not exceeding the management cap).\n  - How each customer\u2019s demand will be allocated across the activated sites.\n  - A cost breakdown showing fixed activation costs and transportation spend.\n  - Site utilization and each site\u2019s share of total system demand to demonstrate compliance with the resilience policy.\n  - Confirmation that the F0\u2013F2 dependency is respected if F0 is selected.\n\nAssumptions and Notes\n- The analysis treats demand as deterministic for the planning window and considers a single aggregated product mix for capacity planning purposes.\n- Distances are calculated consistently from the supplied map coordinates across all candidate\u2013customer pairs.\n- All costs and capacities are applied as provided in the data set.\n- If multiple plans tie on total cost, preference will be given to the one with lower peak utilization and better demand diversification across sites.\n\nNext Steps\n- Validate the data in the Annex and confirm any planning horizon specifics and service-level nuances (e.g., shipment frequency) that could affect capacity or distance interpretations.\n- Run the evaluation across all feasible activation sets and produce the recommended network configuration with a supporting rationale and compliance checks for the policies above.\n\nAnnex: Data Inputs\n- Candidate facility coordinates: Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (77, 66)\n  - F1: (19, 5)\n  - F2: (67, 29)\n  - F3: (16, 12)\n  - F4: (91, 22)\n- Customer coordinates: Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (98, 87)\n  - C1: (46, 15)\n  - C2: (38, 38)\n  - C3: (34, 77)\n  - C4: (0, 27)\n  - C5: (52, 47)\n  - C6: (38, 8)\n  - C7: (5, 38)\n  - C8: (63, 52)\n  - C9: (84, 22)\n  - C10: (2, 4)\n  - C11: (6, 18)\n  - C12: (27, 23)\n  - C13: (64, 24)\n- Facility capacities: [115.0, 198.0, 133.0, 115.0, 195.0]\n- Customer demands: [26.0, 21.0, 28.0, 45.0, 22.0, 23.0, 33.0, 35.0, 39.0, 34.0, 28.0, 50.0, 23.0, 34.0]\n- Fixed opening costs: [223.0, 365.0, 234.0, 386.0, 298.0]\n- Transport rate: 0.5",
